expanded_sketch:
We briefly summarize the strategy to obtain the theorems, focusing on establishing the main theorem. One considers “a one-parameter family of Lie group products $(*_\epsilon)_{\epsilon\in \mathbb{R}}$ on $G$ such that $G=(G,*_1)$ and $(G,*_0)$ is isomorphic to the asymptotic cone of $G$,” and introduces “a one-parameter family of geodesic metrics $(\rho_\epsilon)_{\epsilon\in \mathbb{R}}$ on $G$ such that $\rho_\epsilon$ is $*_\epsilon$-left-invariant for every $\epsilon$, $\rho_1=d$, and $\rho_0=d_\infty$.”

In establishing the main theorem, one deduces it from the following quantitative convergence statement.
\begin{thm}\label{thm: quantitative Pansu}
 Let $G$ be a simply connected nilpotent sub-Finsler Lie group. With respect to an asymptotic grading, consider the Pansu limit metric $\rho_0$ on $G$ and the contracted metrics $\rho_\epsilon$. Then, for every compact set $K \subseteq G$, there is a constant $C>0$ such that
 \begin{equation}\label{eq: thm: quantitative pansu}
  \left|\rho_0(p,q)-\rho_\epsilon(p,q)\right| \leq C \epsilon^{\alpha_{\infty}/\beta}, \quad \forall \epsilon \in [0,1], \; \forall p,q \in K,
 \end{equation}
  where $\alpha_{\infty}$ and $\beta$ are the constants from Definition~\ref{def: constant alpha pansu} and~\ref{def: constant beta}.
\end{thm}

To prove (for fixed compact $K$) “$\rho_0(p,q)-\rho_\epsilon(p,q)\lesssim \epsilon^{\alpha_\infty/\beta}$,” one starts “with a $\rho_\epsilon$-geodesic $\gamma$ from $p$ to $q$,” constructs “a curve $\gamma_0$ such that $\gamma_0(0)=p$ and $\mathrm{Length}_{\rho_0}(\gamma_0)\le \mathrm{Length}_{\rho_\epsilon}(\gamma)$,” and then “bound[s] the $\rho_0$-distance of the endpoints of $\gamma$ and $\gamma_0$.” For that endpoint bound, one uses that “for every Carnot quotient ideal $\mathfrak{i}\triangleleft \mathrm{Lie}(G)$ and for every $\epsilon\in[0,1]$, the curve $t\mapsto \gamma_\epsilon^{-1}(t)*_\epsilon\gamma(t)$ lies in $\mathfrak{i}$,” and then applies “the Ball-Box Theorem and the definition of $\beta$, as well as a Grönwall-type lemma and the definition of $\alpha_\infty$,” to control the $\rho_0$-distance between the endpoints of $\gamma$ and $\gamma_0$. The reverse inequality is proved “using the same techniques.”

