\begin{remark}\label{r-tower}
In our application of Theorem \ref{t-m}, $p_{n+1}/p_n\ge 2$ and are integers (where $p_0=1)$, moreover, $g_n=g_{n+1}^{p_{n+1}/p_n}$ on $I_n$, $n=0,1,...$.
\footnote{It seems, the converse is also true: under the conditions of Theorem \ref{t-m}, $p_{n+1}/p_n\ge 2$ and are integers and $g_n=g_{n+1}^{p_{n+1}/p_n}$ on $I_n$, but we don't need this for the proof.}
In other words, $\mathcal{G}$ is a one-sided infinite tower of maps from the Epstein class. Cf. \cite{mcm1}
where the notion of tower in the context of PL maps was introduced and greatly developed. Theorem \ref{t-m} claims that
a tower of Epstein's maps is a tower of PL maps.
\end{remark}

