To prove the main theorem, they “argue as in \cite{b1} (see also \cite{S}) via known blow-up arguments (\cite{bt}, \cite{det}, \cite{ls}, \cite{llty})”, and one “has to carefully handle the various possibilities arising due to the perturbation (i.e. $\epsilon_n\rightarrow 0^+$) of the homogeneous conical singularity.” The proof is by “contradiction”: “in each one of the blow up scenarios at hand” they must “find at least one bubble coming with some large enough amount of mass.” For this, they need a preliminary “minimal mass” lemma for solutions of
\begin{equation}\label{intro:eq}
 -\D v_n=\left({\epsilon_n^2+|x|^2}\right)^{\alpha_n}V_n(x)e^{\displaystyle v_n} \qquad\text{in} \,\,\, \Omega,
\end{equation}
(proved later), which is then used in the proof of the “$\sup+C\inf$” inequality (done later).

