expanded_sketch:
The proof of the main theorem is described as “a modification of our earlier work \cite{MR4201982},” exploiting that “the Fokker-Planck equation has a distinguished time variable… to handle the non-compact setting,” and obtaining estimates “through a parametrix construction.” (The outline is given for $B=I$.)

Main steps as outlined:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Pull back by the flow of first-order terms.} “We start by pulling the Fokker-Planck equation back by the flow along the first-order terms in
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Qdef}
Q=H_p
+\gamma\sum_{j=1}^J \Im(\bar\ell_j\nabla_x\ell_j)\cdot\partial_\xi
+\frac{\gamma h}2\sum_{j=1}^J (\nabla_x\ell_j\cdot\partial_\xi)(\nabla_x\bar\ell_j\cdot\partial_\xi).
\end{equation}
” In the pulled-back operator, “the leading order terms… are then $\partial_t-(\partial_\xi+t\partial_x)^2$” in the subelliptic order.
\item \emph{Linear change of variables to a H"ormander model.} “It is convenient to make a linear change of variables” to rewrite this as
\[
\partial_t-\tfrac 12\xi\cdot\partial_x-(\partial_\xi+\tfrac 12 t\partial_x)^2,
\]
which is “a H"ormander type operator involving left-invariant vector fields on a step 2 nilpotent Lie group.”
\item \emph{Explicit fundamental solution and leading parametrix term.} “We will see that the fundamental solution on this Lie group can be written explicitly in terms of the Gaussian heat kernel.” This “produces the leading term in a left/right inverse for $\partial_t-Q$.”
\item \emph{Iterate via heat-kernel compositions.} One then “use[s] heat kernel iteration steps to produce a left or right inverse to any desired order,” where “composition of kernels is expressed as convolution in the group product.” The stated local estimate
\begin{multline}\label{eqn:localest}
\sup_{0\le t\le \infty}\,\sum_{|\alpha|+3|\beta|\le 3N}
\|(\eps\partial_\xi)^\alpha(\eps\partial_x)^\beta u(t,\cdot)\|_{L^p(\R^n)}\\
\le 
C_N\sum_{|\alpha|+3|\beta|\le 3N}\|(\eps\partial_\xi)^\alpha(\eps\partial_x)^\beta u_0\|_{L^p(\R^n)}.
\end{multline}
is attributed to these “parametrix mapping properties,” with the $(|\alpha|+3|\beta|)$ counting reflecting the “nonisotropic subelliptic calculus associated to $Q$.”
\item \emph{Global control via limited $t$-differentiation and heat-kernel form.} Because pulling back derivatives yields a frame whose “second derivatives in $t$ increase unboundedly with $\xi$ due to the $\xi\cdot\partial_x$ term in the flow,” “to obtain global estimates we therefore can use only the first few derivatives in $t$,” achieved “by using the heat kernel form of the fundamental solution rather than the pseudodifferential representation used in \cite{MR4201982}.” This underlies the smoothing captured by
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:globalest}
\sup_{T\le t\le \infty}\,\sum_{|\alpha|+3|\beta|\le N}
\|(\eps\partial_\xi)^\alpha(\eps\partial_x)^\beta u(t,\cdot)\|_{L^p(\R^n)}
\le 
C_{N,T}\|u_0\|_{L^p(\R^n)},
\end{equation}
for $t>0$.
\end{itemize}

